select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p1-b09-017","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"In a chart of monthly SEC together with production, the lowest SEC occurred in the month of record production. What does this indicate?","back":"That the plant has fixed energy consumption, i.e. energy used regardless of production level, which is spread over more output when production is high.","explain":"SEC alone (Figure 9.7) showed only a trend. Adding production (Figure 9.8) made the cause clear. Fixed consumption means SEC is not constant and falls as output rises, which is why energy vs production regression is preferred to SEC alone.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p255","order":1440},
{"id":"p1-b09-018","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What is a Moving Annual Total chart and what are its advantages?","back":"Each point is the sum of the previous 12 months of data, so each point covers the full range of seasons and holidays. It also smooths out errors in the timing of meter readings.","explain":"If energy and production Moving Annual Totals track each other, there is no cause for alarm. Any divergence of the energy line is an early warning of waste, or confirmation that efficiency measures are working.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p255","order":1441},
{"id":"p1-b09-019","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"How does linear regression analysis overcome the limitation of time-dependent analysis? Give two typical variable pairs.","back":"It removes the 'time' element and focuses on the variables that influence energy use. Typical pairs: furnace oil vs units of production; electricity vs production; water vs production; lighting electricity vs hours of occupancy.","explain":"Regression establishes standard equations for energy consumption, often from data that would otherwise be meaningless. Table 9.4 gives influencing factors: air volume delivered for compressors, steam generated for boilers, production volume for process steam.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p256","order":1442},
{"id":"p1-b09-020","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"True or false: if a regression analysis shows no significant relationship between energy and a variable, then no relationship exists.","back":"False. Absence of a significant relationship does not necessarily mean none exists. The result depends on the quantity and quality of data and on the variables chosen.","explain":"The book warns that regression depends heavily on data quality and should be treated with care. Poor metering, too few points or a wrong choice of variable can all hide a real relationship.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p256","order":1443},
{"id":"p1-b09-021","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"In an energy vs production XY scatter diagram, what does a low degree of scatter indicate? What does a poor fit indicate when a relationship is known to exist?","back":"Low scatter = good fit. A poor fit where a relationship should exist = poor level of control, and hence potential for energy savings.","explain":"The scatter diagram is the first step in building the energy–production relationship. Wide scatter means the same production is being made with very different amounts of energy, which points to poor operational control.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p257","order":1444},
{"id":"p1-b09-022","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"In the energy–production equation y = c + mx, what do y, x, c and m represent?","back":"y = energy consumed in the period (dependent variable); x = production in the same period (independent variable); c = intercept on the y-axis, i.e. fixed (base-load) energy; m = slope, i.e. energy per unit of production.","explain":"Energy for the period = c + m × production. The best-fit line is found by least squares (minimising the sum of squared distances of points from the line). Once established, it predicts future consumption and serves as the standard for targets and CUSUM.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p258","order":1445},
{"id":"p1-b09-023","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"Write the two 'normal equations' used to find c and m for the best-fit line y = c + mx through n data points.","back":"Σy = n·c + m·Σx and Σxy = c·Σx + m·Σx².","explain":"These come from the least-squares fit. You tabulate x, y, x² and xy, sum each column and solve the two simultaneous equations for c (intercept) and m (slope).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p258","order":1446},
{"id":"p1-b09-024","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"problem","front":"Example 9.1 – A foundry's 9 months of data (production x t/month; energy y toe/month): (380,340), (440,340), (460,380), (520,380), (320,300), (520,400), (240,280), (620,424), (600,420). Find the best-fit line y = c + mx.","back":"Σx = 4100, Σy = 3264, Σx² = 1,994,800, Σxy = 1,537,280 → m ≈ 0.4, c ≈ 180; y = 180 + 0.4x (base load 180 toe/month).","explain":"Solving the normal equations gives the energy–production relationship for the foundry furnace. The intercept, 180 toe/month, is the theoretical base load used regardless of production; each extra tonne costs about 0.4 toe.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p258","steps":[{"prompt":"Tabulate and sum x, y, x² and xy for the 9 months.","answer":"n = 9, Σx = 4100, Σy = 3264, Σx² = 1,994,800, Σxy = 1,537,280.","explain":"These sums are all the normal equations need."},{"prompt":"Write the normal equations.","answer":"9c + 4100m = 3264 and 4100c + 1,994,800m = 1,537,280.","explain":"Σy = nc + mΣx; Σxy = cΣx + mΣx²."},{"prompt":"Eliminate c and solve for m.","answer":"c = (3264 − 4100m)/9. Substituting: 1,486,933 − 1,867,778m + 1,994,800m = 1,537,280 → 127,022m = 50,347 → m ≈ 0.396 ≈ 0.4.","explain":"Substitute c from the first equation into the second."},{"prompt":"Find c and write the line.","answer":"c = (3264 − 4100×0.4)/9 ≈ 180. So y = 180 + 0.4x.","explain":"The book rounds to E = 0.4P + 180, with a theoretical furnace base load of 180 toe/month."}],"order":1447},
{"id":"p1-b09-025","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What does the Pearson correlation coefficient (r) tell you in M&T, and what is its formula?","back":"How well the best-fit line matches the data, i.e. the reliability of the line (1 = 100% correlation). r = Σ(x−x̄)(y−ȳ) / √[Σ(x−x̄)² · Σ(y−ȳ)²].","explain":"Scattered data can still give a regression equation that is meaningless, so r is checked before the equation is used for targets. The book treats r as ranging from 0 to 1. The value is compared with a minimum acceptable r for the number of data points.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p260","order":1448},
{"id":"p1-b09-026","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"problem","front":"For the foundry data of Example 9.1 (x̄ = 455.56 t, ȳ = 362.67 toe), Σ(x−x̄)(y−ȳ) = 50,346.67, Σ(x−x̄)² = 127,022.22 and Σ(y−ȳ)² = 20,832. Find the correlation coefficient and judge it.","back":"r = 50,346.67 / √(127,022.22 × 20,832) ≈ 0.98. This is far above the minimum (~0.8 for 9–10 samples), so the fit is very good.","explain":"An r of 0.98 means production explains energy use almost completely, so the equation E = 0.4P + 180 is reliable for targeting and CUSUM.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p260","steps":[{"prompt":"Multiply the two sums of squares.","answer":"127,022.22 × 20,832 ≈ 2.646 × 10⁹.","explain":"This is the denominator before the square root."},{"prompt":"Take the square root.","answer":"√(2.646 × 10⁹) ≈ 51,441.","explain":"The denominator normalises the covariance term."},{"prompt":"Divide.","answer":"r = 50,346.67 / 51,441 ≈ 0.98.","explain":"Close to 1 means a strong linear relationship."},{"prompt":"Compare with Table 9.5.","answer":"Minimum r for 10 samples is 0.767; 0.98 exceeds it comfortably.","explain":"The book concludes the correlation is very good."}],"order":1449},
{"id":"p1-b09-027","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"Per Table 9.5, what is the minimum acceptable correlation coefficient for 10, 20 and 50 data samples?","back":"10 samples: 0.767; 20: 0.561; 50: 0.362.","explain":"The more data points you have, the lower the r needed to show a real relationship. Other values: 15 → 0.641, 25 → 0.506, 30 → 0.464, 40 → 0.402.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p261","order":1450},
{"id":"p1-b09-028","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What equation describes multi-variable analysis of energy consumption, and how should it be solved?","back":"y = c + m₁x₁ + m₂x₂ + … + mₙxₙ, where x₁…xₙ are the variables influencing y. It is hard to solve by hand, so specialist computer software is advised.","explain":"Energy often depends on several drivers at once (e.g. production and climate). Single-variable analysis uses one independent variable; multi-variable analysis fits a separate coefficient for each driver.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p261","order":1451},
{"id":"p1-b09-029","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"Why are CUSUM charts particularly useful for diagnosing energy problems?","back":"Because they identify the date when energy performance changed, which helps pin-point the problem so its root cause can be analysed.","explain":"CUSUM is the Cumulative Sum of differences between actual and baseline (standard) consumption. The baseline equation is derived from data collected before any intervention, and the CUSUM line changes slope at the moment something changes.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p261","order":1452},
{"id":"p1-b09-030","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"List the steps of CUSUM analysis used in the foundry heat-recovery example.","back":"1) Plot energy vs production for the baseline months; 2) draw the best-fit line; 3) derive its equation; 4) calculate standard (predicted) consumption for each month; 5) find actual − standard; 6) compute the cumulative sum; 7) plot CUSUM vs time; 8) estimate savings.","explain":"These eight steps (asked as 'list at least 5 steps' in the exam) turn raw monthly data into a savings estimate. Only the pre-intervention months are used to build the baseline equation.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p263","order":1453},
{"id":"p1-b09-031","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"On a CUSUM chart (actual − predicted), what does a horizontal line, a rising line and a falling line each mean?","back":"Horizontal: actual and calculated consumption are the same. Rising: performance is worse than standard (specific energy consumption going up). Falling: energy savings are being made.","explain":"A typical CUSUM line oscillates around zero until something changes the consumption pattern, such as an energy saving measure (line falls) or poor control, housekeeping or maintenance (line rises). Site knowledge is needed to link each change of direction to an event.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p262","order":1454},
{"id":"p1-b09-032","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"problem","front":"A foundry furnace (baseline E = 0.4P + 180 toe) got heat recovery during month 9. Months 10–18 (E actual, P t): (400,560), (360,440), (320,360), (340,420), (372,480), (380,540), (280,280), (280,260), (380,500). CUSUM at month 9 is +4. Find the savings.","back":"CUSUM goes +4 (m9) → 0 → +4 (m11) → 0 → −8 → −8 → −24 → −36 → −40 → −40 (m18). Savings ≈ 44 toe over the last 7 months (from +4 to −40), almost 2% of energy use.","explain":"The CUSUM oscillated around zero for a few months and then fell steadily after month 11. The book reads this as the heat recovery system taking about two months to commission, after which steady savings were achieved. Total savings are the drop in CUSUM from where the trend started.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p262","steps":[{"prompt":"Calculate standard consumption E = 0.4P + 180 for months 10–18.","answer":"404, 356, 324, 348, 372, 396, 292, 284, 380 toe.","explain":"The baseline predicts what the furnace would use without the change."},{"prompt":"Find actual − standard for each month.","answer":"−4, +4, −4, −8, 0, −16, −12, −4, 0.","explain":"Negative means less energy than predicted."},{"prompt":"Accumulate starting from +4 at month 9.","answer":"0, +4, 0, −8, −8, −24, −36, −40, −40.","explain":"CUSUM is the running total of the differences."},{"prompt":"Read the savings off the CUSUM.","answer":"From +4 at month 11 to −40 at month 18 = 44 toe.","explain":"The steady downward slope begins after month 11."},{"prompt":"Express as a percentage.","answer":"Actual use in months 12–18 = 2352 toe; 44/2352 ≈ 1.9%, i.e. almost 2%.","explain":"The book quotes 'almost 2% of energy consumption'."}],"order":1455},
{"id":"p1-b09-033","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"problem","front":"Baseline (Jan–Jun 2011): E = 0.5P + 220 toe. Waste heat recovery was installed at end of June. Jul–Dec actual (E toe, P t): (590,760), (605,820), (670,940), (582,750), (512,610), (540,670). Find energy savings and the reduction in SEC.","back":"Predicted: 600, 630, 690, 595, 525, 555. CUSUM: −10, −35, −55, −68, −81, −96. Savings = 96 toe; SEC reduction = 96/4550 ≈ 0.021 toe/t.","explain":"Every month after installation used less energy than the baseline predicted, so the CUSUM falls steadily. Dividing the total saving by the total production for the same six months gives the average drop in specific energy consumption.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p267","steps":[{"prompt":"Compute predicted energy E = 0.5P + 220 for each month.","answer":"Jul 600, Aug 630, Sep 690, Oct 595, Nov 525, Dec 555 toe.","explain":"e.g. July: 0.5×760 + 220 = 600."},{"prompt":"Find actual − predicted.","answer":"−10, −25, −20, −13, −13, −15.","explain":"All negative: savings every month."},{"prompt":"Accumulate to get CUSUM.","answer":"−10, −35, −55, −68, −81, −96.","explain":"The final CUSUM is the total saving."},{"prompt":"Total production Jul–Dec.","answer":"760+820+940+750+610+670 = 4550 t.","explain":"Needed to express the saving per tonne."},{"prompt":"Reduction in SEC.","answer":"96/4550 ≈ 0.021 toe per tonne.","explain":"Saving in toe divided by production in tonnes."}],"order":1456},
{"id":"p1-b09-034","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"problem","front":"A food plant's baseline: energy = 1.8 × production (kg) + 15,000 kWh/month. Measures were taken in June 2010. Jul–Dec (production kg, actual kWh): (62,000, 113,600), (71,000, 139,000), (75,000, 158,000), (59,000, 119,300), (62,000, 123,700), (73,000, 143,600). Use CUSUM to find savings.","back":"Predicted: 126,600; 142,800; 150,000; 121,200; 126,600; 146,400. Differences: −13,000; −3,800; +8,000; −1,900; −2,900; −2,800. CUSUM ends at −16,400, so savings ≈ 16,400 kWh over six months.","explain":"This is the book's end-of-chapter exercise L-1, worked here with the baseline it gives. September used more than predicted (CUSUM rises that month), but the six-month cumulative result is a net saving.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p269","steps":[{"prompt":"Write the baseline.","answer":"E = 1.8P + 15,000 kWh (variable 1.8 kWh/kg plus 15,000 kWh fixed).","explain":"Fixed plus production-related energy."},{"prompt":"Compute predicted energy each month.","answer":"126,600; 142,800; 150,000; 121,200; 126,600; 146,400 kWh.","explain":"e.g. July: 1.8×62,000 + 15,000 = 126,600."},{"prompt":"Actual − predicted.","answer":"−13,000; −3,800; +8,000; −1,900; −2,900; −2,800 kWh.","explain":"A positive value means a worse month."},{"prompt":"Cumulate.","answer":"−13,000; −16,800; −8,800; −10,700; −13,600; −16,400 kWh.","explain":"Running total of the differences."},{"prompt":"State savings.","answer":"Net saving ≈ 16,400 kWh for Jul–Dec 2010.","explain":"The final CUSUM value is the cumulative saving."}],"order":1457},
{"id":"p1-b09-035","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"problem","front":"Measures were implemented before Jan 2011. Production averaged 1000 MT/month in Jan–Jun 2011. Actual vs predicted SEC (kWh/MT): Jan 1203/1121, Feb 1187/1278, Mar 1401/1571, Apr 1450/1550, May 1324/1284, Jun 1233/1233. Use CUSUM to find energy saved.","back":"Differences (actual − predicted): +82, −91, −170, −100, +40, 0. CUSUM: +82, −9, −179, −279, −239, −239 kWh/MT. Savings = 239 kWh/MT × 1000 MT ≈ 2,39,000 kWh.","explain":"This is the book's end-of-chapter exercise L-2, worked here. Because production is a constant 1000 MT/month, the CUSUM of SEC differences can be multiplied directly by monthly production to get the energy saved.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p269","steps":[{"prompt":"Find actual − predicted SEC each month.","answer":"+82, −91, −170, −100, +40, 0 kWh/MT.","explain":"Negative means better than predicted."},{"prompt":"Cumulate.","answer":"+82, −9, −179, −279, −239, −239 kWh/MT.","explain":"The running total is the CUSUM."},{"prompt":"Convert to energy.","answer":"239 kWh/MT × 1000 MT = 2,39,000 kWh.","explain":"Each month's SEC difference applies to 1000 MT."},{"prompt":"Interpret.","answer":"About 2.39 lakh kWh saved in Jan–Jun 2011 (Jan and May were worse than predicted).","explain":"The overall CUSUM trend is downward."}],"order":1458},
{"id":"p1-b09-036","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"List the generic features of commercial Energy Management Information System (EMIS) software.","back":"A database for large long-term data; recording all utility types from meters and invoices; handling complex tariffs; handling related variables (degree days, production); statistical data analysis; quick reporting; and in advanced packages, an interface with Building Management Systems (BMS) for automatic (e.g. hourly) data capture.","explain":"The big advantage is the database, which lets historical data and data from many sources be compared instantly. Under-performing EACs can then be spotted quickly and remedial action taken.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p265","order":1459},
{"id":"p1-b09-037","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"True or false: an EMIS computer package can replace the energy manager in an M&T programme.","back":"False. Computers are simply tools for storing and analysing large amounts of data quickly; they are not a replacement for the energy manager.","explain":"The book is explicit on this point. The software handles data and reports, but interpreting events and starting corrective action stays with people.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p265","order":1460},
{"id":"p1-b09-038","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"How often should M&T reports go to operational managers vs senior management, and what is 'reporting by exception'?","back":"Operational managers may need weekly reports; senior management may need only a quarterly review. Reporting by exception means generating reports only when energy performance falls outside predetermined limits (very poor or very good).","explain":"Monthly reports suit large multi-site organisations, while weekly reports suit complex, high-energy facilities. Too-frequent reporting swamps managers with irrelevant information, which reporting by exception avoids while also cutting everyone's workload.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p266","order":1461},
{"id":"p1-b09-039","paper":1,"topic":"Energy monitoring & targeting","kind":"fact","front":"What qualities should energy management reports have to ensure prompt action?","back":"Simple; highlight where waste is occurring; published regularly; succinct; in a standard format generated automatically by computer; tailored to the reader's level.","explain":"Reports are how action is started in an organisation. A standard automatic format cuts preparation time and makes managers familiar with the information. If the reporting period is too short, though, the system becomes over-complex.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 09 p266","order":1462},
{"id":"p1-088","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"Which six greenhouse gases are covered by the Kyoto Protocol?","back":"Carbon dioxide (CO₂), methane (CH₄), nitrous oxide (N₂O), hydrofluorocarbons (HFCs), perfluorocarbons (PFCs) and sulphur hexafluoride (SF₆).","explain":"CO₂ is the largest contributor by volume. The fluorinated gases have very high Global Warming Potentials. Emissions are reported in tonnes of CO₂-equivalent (tCO₂e) = mass × GWP.","flash":false,"source":"syllabus","order":1463},
{"id":"p1-089","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"Which greenhouse gas has the longest atmospheric lifetime and very high GWP?","back":"Sulphur hexafluoride (SF₆), with an atmospheric lifetime of about 3,200 years and a GWP in the tens of thousands relative to CO₂.","explain":"SF₆ is used as an insulating gas in high-voltage switchgear, so leak prevention and recovery matter. PFCs also last thousands of years. CO₂ has no single lifetime, because it is removed by several processes over very different timescales.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 Q7","order":1464},
{"id":"p1-090","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"What causes stratospheric ozone depletion, and what does the ozone layer filter?","back":"Mainly chlorofluorocarbons (CFCs) and other halogenated compounds. The ozone layer absorbs harmful UV-B radiation.","explain":"Chlorine released from CFCs by UV light destroys ozone catalytically. The Montreal Protocol (1987) phased out CFCs and is phasing out HCFCs, and the Kigali Amendment covers HFCs. Ozone depletion and global warming are separate problems, but some gases contribute to both.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q8; BEE 2024 P1 Q29","order":1465},
{"id":"p1-091","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"What are the basic objectives of sustainable development?","back":"Economic security and prosperity, social development and advancement, and environmental sustainability, all three together.","explain":"The Brundtland definition: development that meets the needs of the present without compromising the ability of future generations to meet their own needs. Energy efficiency supports all three objectives.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q26","order":1466},
{"id":"p1-092","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"What is carbon sequestration?","back":"Capturing CO₂ from point sources (e.g. power plants) or the atmosphere and storing it long term, for example in geological formations, oceans, or biomass and soils.","explain":"Carbon Capture, Utilisation and Storage (CCUS) handles point sources. Forests and soils act as natural carbon sinks.","flash":false,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 Q18","order":1467},
{"id":"p1-093","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"How do you convert fuel carbon content to CO₂ emissions?","back":"CO₂ (kg) = mass of carbon burnt (kg) × 44/12. Each kg of carbon gives 3.67 kg of CO₂.","explain":"The 44/12 comes from molecular weights: CO₂ = 44, C = 12. Example: 1,000 kg of natural gas with 74% carbon → 740 × 3.67 ≈ 2,713 kg CO₂. For electricity, multiply the kWh saved by the grid emission factor, e.g. 1,000 kWh × 0.8 kg/kWh = 800 kg CO₂.","flash":true,"source":"BEE 2025 P1 L6; BEE 2024 P1 Q1","order":1468},
{"id":"p1-094","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"fact","front":"What are India's updated NDC targets under the Paris Agreement (submitted 2022), and its net-zero year?","back":"Reduce the emissions intensity of GDP by 45% by 2030 from the 2005 level; reach about 50% of cumulative installed electric power capacity from non-fossil sources by 2030; net-zero emissions by 2070.","explain":"The Paris Agreement (COP21, 2015) aims to keep warming well below 2°C and to pursue 1.5°C, using nationally determined contributions. It followed the Kyoto Protocol (1997), which set binding targets only for developed countries and created the Clean Development Mechanism.","flash":true,"source":"syllabus","order":1469},
{"id":"p1-095","paper":1,"topic":"Energy & environment","kind":"problem","front":"A 50 MW coal-fired power plant raises its efficiency from 30% to 33% through renovation and modernisation. Coal GCV is 3,600 kcal/kg, the plant runs 8,000 h/year at full load, and the emission factor is 1.5 kg CO₂ per kg of coal. Find the annual coal saving and the CO₂ avoided.","back":"Coal saving ≈ 28,956 t/yr; CO₂ avoided ≈ 43,434 t/yr.","explain":"Heat rate (kcal/kWh) = 860 ÷ efficiency, and specific coal consumption = heat rate ÷ GCV. The fall in specific coal consumption multiplied by generation gives the coal saved, and the emission factor converts that to CO₂ avoided. A 3-point efficiency gain cuts fuel use by about 9%.","steps":[{"prompt":"Specific coal consumption before.","answer":"Heat rate = 860/0.30 = 2,866.7 kcal/kWh; SCC = 2,866.7/3,600 = 0.7963 kg/kWh","explain":"SCC = 860 ÷ (η × GCV)."},{"prompt":"Specific coal consumption after.","answer":"860/0.33 = 2,606.1 kcal/kWh; SCC = 0.7239 kg/kWh","explain":""},{"prompt":"Annual generation.","answer":"50,000 kW × 8,000 h = 4×10⁸ kWh","explain":"Energy = capacity × hours."},{"prompt":"Coal saved.","answer":"(0.7963 − 0.7239) × 4×10⁸ = 2.896×10⁷ kg ≈ 28,956 t/yr","explain":"Saving = ΔSCC × generation."},{"prompt":"CO₂ avoided.","answer":"28,956 × 1.5 = 43,434 t CO₂/yr","explain":"CO₂ = coal saved × emission factor."}],"flash":false,"source":"BEE 2024 P1 S2 (modified)","order":1470},
{"id":"p1-b10-001","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Name the six principal emissions from fuel combustion that affect the air environment.","back":"Carbon dioxide, particulate matter (dust), sulphur oxides, nitrogen oxides, hydrocarbons and carbon monoxide.","explain":"These are by-products of burning hydrocarbon fuels in industry. CO₂ dominates total emissions by quantity, while SOx, NOx and particulates are the main local and regional air pollutants.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p273","order":1471},
{"id":"p1-b10-002","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"What is the main source of each: particulate matter, SOx, NOx and CO from combustion?","back":"Particulates: ash content of solid fuels (coal, lignite, biomass). SOx: sulphur in oil and coal. NOx: nitrogen from both the fuel and the combustion air. CO: incomplete combustion.","explain":"SOx and NOx are flagged as major global air pollutants because they cause acid rain, a trans-boundary problem. CO points directly to poor combustion, which is also an efficiency loss.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p273","order":1472},
{"id":"p1-b10-003","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"True or false: carbon dioxide is classed as an air pollutant in the BEE guide book.","back":"False. CO₂ is not considered a pollutant, but it is a major contributor to global warming and climate change.","explain":"CO₂ comes from the oxidation of carbon in fuel and dominates total combustion emissions. Its concern is climatic rather than toxic, which is why it is treated under global warming, not air pollution.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p273","order":1473},
{"id":"p1-b10-004","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Name the four key environmental issues of global significance. Is suspended particulate matter one of them?","back":"Acid rain, ozone layer depletion, global warming and climate change, and loss of biodiversity. Suspended particulate matter is not a global issue (it is a local one).","explain":"Global issues affect all mankind regardless of country, so they need international effort. That raises the question of who should do what, which is the basis of agreements such as UNFCCC and Kyoto.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p273","order":1474},
{"id":"p1-b10-005","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"How is acid rain formed, and name four of its effects.","back":"SOx and NOx from fossil fuel combustion mix with water vapour to form sulphuric and nitric acids. Effects: acidification of lakes, streams and soils; release of metals such as aluminium that wash away plant nutrients; killing of trees, crops and aquatic life; decay of buildings, paints and statues; respiratory, skin and eye problems.","explain":"Deposition can be wet (rain, snow, sleet) or dry (particles and gases). Because the pollutants travel far before falling, acid rain crosses national borders.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p274","order":1475},
{"id":"p1-b10-006","paper":1,"topic":"Energy efficiency & climate change","kind":"fact","front":"Where is the ozone layer, and at what UV wavelengths is ozone naturally formed and destroyed?","back":"In the stratosphere, about 10–50 km above the earth. Ozone forms when O₂ absorbs UV below 240 nm and is destroyed when it absorbs UV above 290 nm.","explain":"Natural formation and destruction used to be in balance. The layer blocks the sun's UV-B radiation; depletion lets more harmful radiation reach the surface.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 1 Ch 10 p274","order":1476}
]$cards$::jsonb);
